preConditionHook:
// BROOKS'S UP-TICK DIVERGENCE (V10-03). After a recent volume peak -- the
// climax bar the book tells the reader to remember -- price makes one more
// new extreme on far LESS volume than the peak carried. That is the bull
// pullback signal in a bear trend: a lower low, but the sellers are gone.
// LONG is the lower low on light volume; SHORT is the mirror, a higher high
// on light volume.
//
// 'Remember the huge-volume bar' is mechanized as Highest(volume, lookback)[1]
// -- the largest volume of the N bars ENDING ONE BAR AGO, so the current bar's
// own volume can never be its own benchmark. That needs no cross-bar state at
// all, which is why no climax latch is carried. Brooks's 'one or two lower
// lows on less volume' is taken at its first term, a single lower-low bar; a
// second leg is a separate placement.
//
// THE ENGINE ALIAS HERE IS UP-TICKS, NOT TOTAL. The register's `volume` row is VERIFIED:
// EasyLanguage's volume word equals UpTicks on all 18,203 measured bars of @KW
// 480min and 1440min, because a 1440-minute bar is still an INTRADAY bar
// (BarInterval reads 1440). Both the peak and the current reading are that
// same up-tick series, so the ratio is internally consistent -- but it is an
// up-tick ratio, not a total-volume ratio, and a rule wanting total ticks
// would have to say ctx.Ticks (as VolumeAboveAverageMultiple does).
//
// Highest bottoms out in EL's Extremes(), EasyLanguage SOURCE, so it carries
// the relational tolerance -- hence el_gt in the scan and not a raw >. The
// window is indices 1..lookback, exactly Highest(volume, lookback)[1].
// volume is cast to double before any arithmetic, as VolumeBelowAverage does.
peakVolume = (double)volume[1];
for (int i = 2; i <= (int)lookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt((double)volume[i], peakVolume)) peakVolume = (double)volume[i];
}
